      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The majority of upper extremity surgeries are performed on an ambulatory basis under intravenous regional anaesthesia or brachial plexus blockade. The former technique is easy to perform, has a rapid onset and a high success rate but provides limited post-operative analgesia. Brachial plexus blockade provides excellent intraoperative anaesthesia as well as post-operative analgesia, eliminates the need for post-operative opioids, resulting in a decrease in recovery time, shortened hospital stay, increased patient satisfaction and ultimately a decrease in perioperative costs when compared with general anaesthesia. This chapter reviews upper extremity surgical procedures performed below the shoulder, the anaesthetic options available, and techniques used to optimize post-operative pain control."},
